Tests
=====

Unit tests use Catch2 (v3) and run through CMake's ctest:

    cmake -S . -B build -DENABLE_COVERAGE=ON
    cmake --build build
    cmake --build build --target coverage

The `coverage` target runs ctest, writes `build/junit.xml` (JUnit, used by
the GitLab `unittests` job) and `build/coverage.xml` (Cobertura, via gcovr).
Without `-DENABLE_COVERAGE=ON` only `ctest --test-dir build
--output-junit junit.xml` is needed. If Catch2 is not installed the tests
are skipped silently; `-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF` disables them explicitly.

Besides the Catch2 unit tests there are end to end tests that run the
program on `test/data/synthetic.osm` (OSM XML, so diffs stay readable in
git) and compare the result with the `test/data/expected_*.json` golden
files for `-e`, `-m` and `--nocache`. The input is generated by
`test/data/gen_synthetic.py`; after an intended output change regenerate
the golden files by running the program on it with the same flags.

Design decisions
================

* **JSON output is always produced by nlohmann/json, never hand written.**
  The input data (OSM tags such as street names, housenumbers and
  postcodes) is heavily untrusted: it can contain quotes, backslashes,
  control characters or invalid UTF-8. Correct escaping and number
  formatting is security relevant, so it is left to a well tested library
  instead of a home grown encoder. A hand rolled serializer is measurably
  faster (about 1s of 19s with `-e` on `owl.pbf`), but this is not worth
  the risk and maintenance burden and is deliberately not done.


